     Actually, let[0m[2m me think more carefully. The loop iterates over `self._jobs.values()`. When worker 1 finds job 1 (status "pending"),[0m[2m it then hits `await asyncio.sleep(0.01)` which gives control to the event loop. Worker 2 then runs its `dequeue()`,[0m[2m also iterates over `self._jobs.values()` and can find the same job 1 still with[0m[2m status "pending" because worker 1 hasn't set it to "processing" yet. After the sleep, worker 1 sets status to "processing" and[0m[2m returns the job.
     
     But wait - when worker 2 resumes and continues its loop, what happens? Worker 2 is in[0m[2m the middle of `for job in self._jobs.values():`. The dictionary iteration... In Python 3.[0m[2m7+, dictionaries maintain insertion order. But when you iterate and another coroutine modifies the dictionary, you can[0m[2m get issues.
     
